## Context

Decisions that are expensive to reverse get lost in chat and PR threads, and the next person, or the client who
inherits this repository, cannot tell what was decided, by whom, or why.

## Decision

Each one-way decision gets an ADR here, numbered in sequence: a change that, after it has been live, reverting and
redeploying would not undo (data reshaped or dropped, a contract others depend on, data that crossed a trust or
tenancy boundary, an effect that cannot be called back). Reversible decisions are logged and confirmed in a batch
instead. The named decider accepts an ADR; an accepted ADR is never edited, a new one supersedes it.

## Consequences

Every ADR states its Status and Deciders, and CI rejects one that does not. Writing an ADR costs a screenful; arguing
one in a proposed PR first is optional.
